<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Julius II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1443">1443</a>–<a href="/years/1513">1513</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Giuliano della Rovere · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Julius II, born Giuliano della Rovere, was pope from 1503 to 1513 and known as the 'warrior pope'. He reclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>'s fall, personally led campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1506 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> in 1511, and gave Urbino and Pesaro to his nephew. He commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> ceiling and began the new St Peter's Basilica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Julius II" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raphael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Julius_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parma and Piacenza, two prosperous cities of the Po plain on the Via Emilia, had belonged to the Duchy of Milan for much of the late Middle Ages before <b>Pope Julius II</b> annexed them to the Papal States in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Julius II</b>, who founded the Swiss Guard in 1506, personally led his army in winter 1511 and entered the captured town of Mirandola through a breach in its walls.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Julius II</b> laid the first stone of Bramante's new basilica in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Julius II</b>, Louis XII of France, Emperor Maximilian I and Ferdinand of Aragon formally concluded the League of Cambrai against Venice.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1511 <b>Pope Julius II</b> personally led the siege of the fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mirandola">Duchy of Mirandola</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Julius II</b>, a Borgia enemy, reclaimed the towns for the Papal States, while Venice seized Rimini and Faenza.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-romagna">Duchy of Romagna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Albisola Superiore, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.34,8.51&amp;year=1488" aria-label="Albisola Superiore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4754 595 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Julius II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5204 865h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5204 865h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.34,8.51&amp;year=1488">What was here in 1488? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
